00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.020
PROFESSOR: So today is
the human power lab.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.770
And we're going to start
with a lab safety tour

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.210
that Gwyn is going to run.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.977
This is Gwyn Jones,
who's a D lab instructor.

00:00:36.977 --> 00:00:39.310
He's teaching D lab design
with a couple of other people

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:42.880
this semester and has a
lot of shop experience

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.320
and a good person to have
as a resource around.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.940
So he'll do the safety tour.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.860
Once you have the safety
tour, then you guys

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.780
are free to use the
lab after hours,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.300
which is a nice resource.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.840
And then after that, we're
going to split up into teams,

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.430
try to divide the class evenly.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.000
And I think we're at
about 16 students, so five

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.010
or six students per team.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.088
And each group is going
to do a different thing.

00:01:09.088 --> 00:01:10.630
And then we're going
to switch twice,

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.160
so that everyone
does everything.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.130
So the three options
are machining with Gwyn

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:18.010
in the D lab lab,
which I think is 102--

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.690
but that might be a lie--

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.900
water carrying, which will
start up here with me,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.440
and water pumping, which will
be outside in the driveway,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.835
outside the D lab.

00:01:27.835 --> 00:01:30.370
So make sure you know where
your coats are if you're not

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.210
wearing a sweater.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.400
Because it's a
little chilly out.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.530
And then we'll rotate.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.400
And hopefully, we'll get
done a little early today,

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.660
but not a ton early.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.890
So we'll see.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:53.690
There's five of you.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:55.520
So one person is going
to be the spotter,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.353
because some of these
water carrying methods

00:01:57.353 --> 00:01:59.180
are rather challenging.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.840
Because the tour that you're
going to do four times,

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.350
five, one is a spotter,
to do all the attempts,

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:10.070
you're going to go down
this hall, down the stairs,

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:13.820
outside, cross the street,
and then come back.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:17.840
And so that's to experience
flat surface, nice and easy,

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.350
stairs, more or less easy
depending on the method,

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:22.700
and then some rough terrain.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.410
So that's the plan.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:34.100
And the methods are
traditional American style,

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.760
a method that's common primarily
for women all over the world.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.650
And I may need help
to get this up.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.817
Yeah, I'm totally going to
need help to get this up.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.540
So you just work
to get it centered.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.900
And then hypothetically, you
can do this with no hands.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.383
I've yet to meet any D
labber who can do that.

00:02:56.383 --> 00:02:57.925
But you meet plenty
of people who can

00:02:57.925 --> 00:02:59.750
in developing countries.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.180
But try to get to one
hand when you're doing it.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.780
And if you see
people who have been

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.120
doing this their whole
lives, not only--

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.290
Oh, that was really bad.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.720
Good thing we have two.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.820
Not only can they
do it with no hands.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.890
But your head has a
point in the middle.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.433
So they'll start on one side.

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:23.600
And then when they get
tired, they just go like that

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.980
and switch to the other side.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:29.910
And it works, which I
don't know how you do that.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.310
So that's two methods.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.950
Number three is called
a temp line, which

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.070
is used in many developing
countries as well,

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:40.250
often in the same places
they do head carries.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:44.360
And so for this one, it
goes on your forehead.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.150
And it uses your neck.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.440
And you play with it to get
it comfortable on your neck.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.210
In areas where this
is really common

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:56.120
and people are carrying one to
two times their body weight,

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.700
you see most of their
foreheads have ridges.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.550
Because it just digs in,
because you're carrying

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.470
from a pretty young age.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:08.470
And then the last method
is called the Q drum, which

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:12.613
you can see here, which
was invented-- actually,

00:04:12.613 --> 00:04:14.280
there's two different
groups that do it.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:15.030
One is the Q drum.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.640
One is the hippo roller.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.230
And the idea is to help kids
carry water very far distances.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:24.740
So a lot of people are carrying
water many miles, or at least

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.330
a mile, every single day.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:28.860
They're usually kids
and women doing it.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.880
And so we're not even going
to come close to a mile.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.240
And we're generally carrying
less water than they are.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:36.950
And we ate a much
better breakfast

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.750
and got a much better rest
and are in much better health.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.788
So it will be a little
annoying, but not as annoying

00:04:42.788 --> 00:04:44.330
as it would be if
this was your life.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:46.310
So it's a little snapshot.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.230
So come on around.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:48.980
Let's get started.

00:04:51.627 --> 00:04:52.960
That actually looks pretty good.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.220
Let it go down step by step.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.560
AUDIENCE: The crown on beach.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:37.030
Hi, everyone.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.104
Oh, there's a [INAUDIBLE]
you can see it.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.670
PROFESSOR: What we kind of want
the point of this module to be

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.760
is for you to understand how
much power we can generate

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:12.950
with your arms versus legs.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.620
And so this is a pump
that uses your legs.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.300
And that's a pump
that uses your arms.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.258
Everyone here is going
to use both pumps to try

00:06:19.258 --> 00:06:27.490
to fill the bucket and see how
much power you can generate.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.360
This pump was a redesign on
a plastic injection molded

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.850
pump that was done by the
full grout eye project

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:34.900
by Josh Brandes.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.760
And he made the entire
thing out of cement.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.010
It's the way more applicable
for the developing world.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.470
So you can measure power by
pressure times volumetric flow.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:49.810
So we're measuring
volumetric flow

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:53.650
by how quickly we can fill a
five gallon bucket with water.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:55.720
And the way we're
measuring pressure

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.670
is we're going to use
the hydrostatic pressure

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:59.805
equation, which is RHO GH.

00:06:59.805 --> 00:07:01.930
And so we're going to
measure the height that we're

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:03.910
pumping the water to and
then just multiply it

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.410
by the density of
water and gravity.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.053
OK, you ready?

00:07:11.053 --> 00:07:11.920
AUDIENCE: Go for it.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:12.503
PROFESSOR: Go.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.930
AUDIENCE: I think
it's like stuck.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:31.820
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.320
AUDIENCE: Maybe take it
out of the water, like--

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.820
There you go.

00:07:42.238 --> 00:07:43.530
AUDIENCE: Yeah, this is tiring.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:44.030
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.072
AUDIENCE: It's a workout.

00:08:15.956 --> 00:08:17.930
AUDIENCE: That's good.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:18.620
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:19.287
PROFESSOR: 2:45.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:28.280
Oh, what happens is when
water pushes down on--

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.320
water gets pushed
down this way, it

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:33.559
pushes the marble against that
inner lip and creates a seal.

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:36.054
And then on the top
you have a PVC pipe

00:08:36.054 --> 00:08:37.429
that has either
a nail through it

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:39.840
or is bent to close
it in some way.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.890
So that when water
gets pushed this way,

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:45.290
it pushes against this lip.

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:47.120
And then water can
flow around the marble.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:48.953
And so it creates
a one way valve.

00:08:48.953 --> 00:08:50.870
And it's actually a
really good one way valve.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.600
And the cool thing about it
is it gets better with time,

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:57.110
because the plastic bends around
to the shape of the marble,

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:00.410
instead of other one way valves,
which get much worse with time.

00:09:08.008 --> 00:09:09.300
AUDIENCE: Is everything set up?

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:10.200
AUDIENCE: Yep.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.950
As long as you have
enough clearance here.

